    <briefdescription>
<para>Internal error codes used by NDXL, what they represent, when they are raised, and how to query and respond to them. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>NDXL distinguishes between function return values and error codes.</para>
<para>Function return values indicate whether an operation succeeded:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>For functions returning numeric values:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>NDXL_OK indicates success.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>NDXL_ERR indicates failure.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>For functions returning pointers:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Non-NULL indicates success.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>NULL (0) indicates failure.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>The specific cause of an error is recorded separately and can be retrieved using <ref refid="group__ndxl__err_1ga431ccb893f54f2ce7fe5ead2a8abd3cf" kindref="member">ndxlGetLastErrorCode()</ref>, while its associated human-readable description can be retrieved using <ref refid="group__ndxl__err_1ga7ed0ec0987f06de2cc78458f0f150002" kindref="member">ndxlGetLastErrorMsg()</ref>.</para>
<para>The error codes NDXL_VALUE_ERR, NDXL_ARG_ERR, and NDXL_SIM_ERR are exclusively used to identify the cause of an error and are never returned directly by functions.</para>
<para>A formatted string combining both pieces of information can be directly printed using <ref refid="group__ndxl__err_1ga2091f6acf85227ca08a16369595c5bff" kindref="member">ndxlPrintError()</ref>.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="warning"><para>Error information is stored globally and shared between all threads and objects. Consequently, an error raised by one thread or object may overwrite error information previously recorded by another thread or object. Periodic use of error querying while developing a simulation is strongly recommended. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
